`useEffect` synchronizes a component with something outside React, such as a browser event, timer, connection, or network request. It runs after React commits a render. If it returns a cleanup function, React calls that cleanup before rerunning the effect with changed dependencies and when the component unmounts. [React reference](https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect)

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const connection = connect(roomId);
  return () => connection.disconnect();
}, [roomId]);
```

The dependency list controls when it reruns: no list means after every render; `[]` means after mounting; `[roomId]` means after mounting and when `roomId` changes. In development, Strict Mode may run an extra setup and cleanup cycle to expose missing cleanup. [React reference](https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect)

**Common mistakes and better choices:**

- **Missing dependencies:** This can leave the effect using old props or state. Include every reactive value it reads, and use the React Hooks linter. [Removing Effect Dependencies](https://react.dev/learn/removing-effect-dependencies)
- **Missing cleanup:** Remove listeners, clear timers, and disconnect subscriptions. For requests, handle cancellation or stale responses so an older result cannot overwrite a newer one. [React reference](https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect)
- **Using an effect for derived values:** Calculate values from props and state during render; use `useMemo` if a calculation is measurably expensive. [You Might Not Need an Effect](https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect)
- **Using an effect for a user action:** Put code caused by a click or submit in that event handler. [You Might Not Need an Effect](https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect)

A useful test: **what external system needs to stay in sync with this component?** If there isnΓÇÖt one, you probably donΓÇÖt need `useEffect`.
